<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Italy</p><h1 class="st-title">Commune of Orvieto</h1><p class="st-meta">1157 CE – 1354 CE (approx.) · capital Orvieto</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Guelph hill-town republic on a volcanic crag in Umbria, a frequent papal residence and builder of one of Italy's most splendid Gothic cathedrals.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Orvieto, perched on a tufa cliff above the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paglia">Paglia</a> valley, had consuls by 1157. The commune extended its rule over much of the surrounding countryside towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-bolsena">Lake Bolsena</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maremma">Tuscan Maremma</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Orvieto was ruled by consuls, then a podestà and a popular government of the Seven. Strongly Guelph, it often hosted popes in the 13th century, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-urban-iv">Urban IV</a>, at whose court <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-aquinas">Thomas Aquinas</a> taught. The cathedral, begun in 1290, was linked to the Eucharistic miracle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolsena">Bolsena</a> and the feast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corpus-christi-feast">Corpus Christi</a>; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luca-signorelli">Luca Signorelli</a> later painted its frescoes. The Guelph Monaldeschi and Ghibelline Filippeschi, both named by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dante-alighieri">Dante</a>, fought for control.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Weakened by faction and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-death">Black Death</a>, the commune fell to strongmen in the 1340s. In 1354 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gil-alvarez-carrillo-de-albornoz">Cardinal Albornoz</a> restored papal authority, and Orvieto remained part of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/papal-states">Papal States</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="city state, 1157 CE – 1354 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M24 82 V42 H32 V34 H40 V42 H46 V34 H54 V42 H60 V34 H68 V42 H76 V82 Z"/><path d="M44 82 V66 Q50 58 56 66 V82"/><path d="M34 54 H40 M60 54 H66"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">city state</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Italy</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:82.73%;width:3.92%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1157 CE – 1354 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>city state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Orvieto</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Italian, Latin</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Orvieto</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1256&amp;focus=commune-of-orvieto" aria-label="Commune of Orvieto on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4973 660 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Commune of Orvieto lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5293 916L5304 942"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5304 942h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5293 916h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5293 916h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Orvieto. <a href="/atlas?year=1256&amp;focus=commune-of-orvieto">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
